# Qoduck — findings by MiniMax M3.1 Flash

- Source: Qoder / Alibaba (`opencode/qoduck`)
- Date: 2026-11-12 (UTC)
- Overview and scoring methodology: `../../model-comparison.md`
- Cross-model signed log: `../../model-findings.md`

> **Self-exclusion notice.** No verified public benchmark number exists for this model id. Every benchmark row below reads "no verified public score found", so this file is intentionally saved as `MiniMax_M3.1_Flash.md.excluded` and contributes nothing to the averages. What follows is the verified record of what Qoduck is, where it ran, when it ran and what is publicly unknown.

## Model card

- **Name:** Qoduck — the name under which an **undisclosed stealth model** appeared in Alibaba's Qoder coding product on 2026-11-07. The vendor's own documentation calls it "a stealth model whose identity has yet to be disclosed"; Alibaba Cloud's announcement told users "Don't ask where it came from."
- **Short description:** A short-lived, free-tier stealth model inside the Qoder IDE/agent product, positioned as a high-reasoning coding and repository-scale refactoring model. It was never named by Alibaba, never exposed through a public API, never published as weights, and was scheduled for removal when its promotion window closed.
- **Provider / access:** **Qoder International only** (Qoder's desktop/web product, model picker entry "Qoduck"). Qoder's promotion page states explicitly that the promotion "applies to Qoder International" and that the Qoder IDE, JetBrains plugin and Qoder CLI "currently do not support the promotion". There is **no public API route**: no OpenCode Zen id, no OpenRouter id, no Vercel AI Gateway id, no models.dev row and no Hugging Face repository exist for it.
- **Release / knowledge:** First sighted **2026-11-07** (Qoder event page and Alibaba Cloud's announcement on the same day). The promotion ran **from 2026-11-07 to 2026-11-11 at 10:00 (UTC+8)**, and Qoder's own FAQ states "Qoduck will be removed when the promotion ends" — i.e. the route was scheduled to disappear the day before this report was written. No knowledge cutoff is published.
- **IDs:** `Qoduck` (the label in Qoder's model picker); the repository's curated entry assigns `opencode/qoduck`, but **that id does not exist** in OpenCode's live inference catalogue, in the Zen model/pricing/deprecation tables, in models.dev, in OpenRouter or in the Vercel AI Gateway catalogue (all checked 2026-11-12).
- **Context window:** **unverified.** The repository's curated entry claims 1,048,576 total tokens with 65,536 max output; the only public corroboration is a third-party tracker (stealthmodels.com, 2026-11-07) reporting that users "can switch it to a one-million-token window". Qoder's own documentation does not publish a context size. Treat 1M as a reported, not vendor-confirmed, figure.
- **Modalities:** **unverified.** Qoder's documentation describes the model only as a model in the picker with adjustable "thinking intensity" (the promo applies "even at the highest thinking intensity"); it publishes no modality list. The repository's curated entry claims text and image in with text and code out — no public source confirms this, and no image input is documented anywhere.
- **Pricing (as of 2026-11-12):** Priced in **Qoder Credits, not tokens**: the standard rate is **0.5x credits** per call, and inside the daily free allowance the rate drops to **0.0x** so calls consume no credits — this applied "even at the highest thinking intensity", and it did not draw on the 100 daily general-purpose credits. The repository's curated entry additionally claims a "standard OpenCode Zen rate", which cannot be verified because no Zen route exists. A third-party tracker reports the normal Qoder price "matches Qwen3.8-Max", i.e. no bespoke model was priced.
- **Architecture:** **unverified and undisclosed.** No parameter count, architecture description, weights, tokenizer fingerprint from the vendor, or model card exists. The only technical signal is third-party: a tokenizer probe reported by stealthmodels.com places the model in the **Qwen family**, and an anonymous tester reported that it "beat every released Qwen on a knowledge check" — an unquantified claim with no harness, no sample and no published number.

